==English==

===Verb===
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{present participle of|en|hanker|nocat=1}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1861|author={{w|Anthony Trollope}}|title=Framley Parsonage|chapter=4|passage=One may say that '''hankering''' after naughty things is the very essence of the evil into which we have been precipitated by Adam's fall.}}
#* '''2008''' May 23, James Graff, "[https://web.archive.org/web/20120911145241/http://www.time.com/time/world/article/0,8599,1808953,00.html Lost: Labour's Love for Brown]," ''Time'':
#*: [T]here is a clear sense that Britain is '''hankering''' for a change at the top.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|often|followed by {{m|en|for}} or {{m|en|after}}}} A strong, [[restless]] [[desire]], [[longing]], or mental [[inclination]].
#: {{syn|en|craving}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1840|author={{w|Washington Irving}}|title=The Knight of Malta|passage=I found that he had dipped a little in chimerical studies and had a '''hankering''' after astrology and alchymy.}}
#* '''1848''', {{w|William Makepeace Thackeray}}, ''Vanity Fair'', Chapter 24:
#*: "{{...}} You don't mean," Mr. Osborne continued, gathering wrath and astonishment as the thought now first came upon him; "you don't mean that he's such a d—— fool as to be still '''hankering''' after that swindling old bankrupt's daughter? {{...}}"
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Shirley|chapter=1|passage=Mike says he even likes to talk to him and run after him, but he has a '''hankering''' that Moore should be made an example of.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=1899|month=Feb|author=Joseph Conrad|authorlink=Joseph Conrad|title=The Heart of Darkness|journal=Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine|page=197|passage=But there was one yet - the biggest, the most blank, so to speak - that I had a '''hankering''' after.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1904|author={{w|W. W. Jacobs}}|title=Dialstone Lane|chapter=2|passage="Some people are fond of a stay-at-home life, but I always had a '''hankering''' after adventures."}}
#* '''2010''' Aug. 12, Michael D. Lemonick, "[https://web.archive.org/web/20120913163235/http://www.time.com/time/health/article/0,8599,2009968,00.html Study: Lucy's Relatives Used Tools to Butcher Meat]," ''Time'':
#*: In other words, some species of human ancestor . . . not only had a '''hankering''' for meat, which scientists had not expected, but used tools to get it.

